WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.100
- [Narrator] This Barnes &amp; Noble

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.690
in Pikesville, Maryland used to be

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.440
in a 24,000 square foot
space in this building.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.930
Now it's moved across
the street to a space

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.660
that's less than half the size,

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.180
and with the downsizing came a new look

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.550
and new books chosen specifically

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.530
for this store's customers.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.500
It's all part of Barnes &amp;
Noble's strategy to tailor each

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.190
of the company's more than
600 locations to local tastes.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.590
- Barnes &amp; Noble is adopting the strategy

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.530
of independent bookstores that
it once put out of business.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:32.640
- [Narrator] Here's how the bookseller

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.470
is implementing its new approach

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.160
and why it thinks this new
strategy will sell more books.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.127
This is "The Economics of Barnes &amp; Noble."

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.560
Barnes &amp; Noble was first founded in 1873.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.740
In 1971, Leonard Riggio
acquired the company,

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.780
which set off a period of growth.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.150
The retailer went public in 1993

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.400
in an era when it was opening superstores

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.020
across the country.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.330
- In the 1990s and 2000s,

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.430
Barnes &amp; Noble bookstores really looked

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.980
like large supermarkets filled with books,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.900
usually anchoring strip centers

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.210
in the suburbs or rural areas.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.240
- [Narrator] And whether in
Phoenix or Maryland or Ohio,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.770
the stores had the same books

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.350
with the same furniture
and the same layout.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.540
- It either had one floor or it had two.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.170
If it had two, it had
enormous escalators going up

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.780
with this huge sort of
open space in the middle

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.010
to an upper floor.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.800
- [Narrator] That model happened
to be extremely scalable.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.870
- They were trying to fill their stores

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.410
with books and infrastructure
at the lowest cost possible,

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.410
so they wanted to order all
the same types of bookshelves,

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.090
the same type of furnishings

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.790
and decorations and other accoutrements.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.430
- [Narrator] So the
company grew, and fast.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.590
Those huge stores carried more books

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.320
at lower prices than
independent bookstores.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.200
- [Interviewee] We all remember
the rom com "You Got Mail."

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.484
- Joe Fox. I'm in the book business.

00:01:54.484 --> 00:01:55.800
- I am in the book business.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.350
- So many closed when
Barnes &amp; Noble came to town

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.260
and they weren't able to
compete with the title selection

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.480
or prices that Barnes &amp; Noble offered.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.550
- [Narrator] The big stores
and large inventories

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.910
weren't enough to save Barnes
&amp; Noble from a digital threat.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.240
- Amazon was able to offer,
kind of like Barnes &amp; Noble had

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.630
in the past, a huge selection
of titles at lower prices

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.270
than Barnes &amp; Noble could
because it didn't have the cost

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:19.103
of real estate

00:02:19.103 --> 00:02:22.170
and other costs that Barnes
&amp; Noble was shouldering.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.370
- [Narrator] And it sunk a lot

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.490
of money trying to keep
pace, particularly in NOOK,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:27.930
its e-reader business.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:31.080
- [Announcer] Take your story
wherever you want it to go.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.210
- [Narrator] But nothing
helped stop the bleeding.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.900
The company's revenue
has declined since 2012.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.460
Then in 2019,

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.670
hedge fund Elliott Management
Corporation acquired the chain

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:42.990
and installed James Daunt

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.790
who led British Retailer Waterstones

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.530
and founded his own independent bookstore,

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.720
Daunt Books, as CEO.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.120
- It felt entirely intuitive to me

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.190
that you allow each bookstore to run

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:54.390
as an independent bookstore.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.150
They will create much more
interesting, much more dynamic,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.100
much more engaging bookstores.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.510
- [Narrator] And that
meant reminding people

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:04.050
about the appeal of in-person
versus online shopping.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.630
- What you're missing is
the opportunity to kind

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.930
of read the book flaps of all
the other books that are there

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.030
and maybe get recommendations

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.090
from the local bookseller
who's working there.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:15.923
- [Narrator] The company says

00:03:15.923 --> 00:03:17.340
it wanted to emphasize the experience

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.680
of visiting a physical bookstore

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.590
so its new strategy strips
stores of the cookie cutter look

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.320
and feel that had helped
the chain grow its revenue

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.640
and store count.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.140
Some locations have moved on

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.690
from the big box model to smaller stores.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.040
- And in these smaller spaces,

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.260
they are reconfiguring the stores

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.640
so that they're more of maze

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.710
and less of a supermarket aisle.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.290
- [Narrator] Plus, the company
says each individual store

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:41.400
has more autonomy

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.640
over what books to order
and how to display them.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.620
Here at the Pikesville, Maryland location,

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.330
the store manager says that customers

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:52.020
like romance, books from Black
authors and lots of fiction.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.900
So the large romance section
is at the front of the store

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.340
and there's a huge display right

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.010
in the center celebrating
Black History Month.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.010
Store managers can also
choose how to run promotions,

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:04.290
like this buy one, get one 50% off deal

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.200
on specific fiction books
chosen by the team here.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.300
- Brooklyn has a very
different demographic,

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.180
very different set of
customers, a very different set

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.230
of book interests to let's say somebody

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.650
in Alabama, in Georgia.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.090
Who is the best person

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:23.970
to judge what those particular
communities wanna buy?

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.460
It's the booksellers in those communities.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.200
- [Narrator] The company
has also been listening

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.930
to its store managers on wider trends.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.090
- Manga was a small
part of our assortment.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:36.210
We had maybe five, six
bays of books on manga,

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.740
and yet when these booksellers

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.150
were reorganizing their stores,

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.180
they were coming back to us
saying, we want to do 38.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.770
You know, we want to do 42,

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:47.400
which was just almost
sort of incomprehensible,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.470
except that's what they
said their customers

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:50.640
would respond to.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.920
- [Narrator] Daunt also used
this localizing strategy

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.470
to turn around British
bookstore chain Waterstones,

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:58.680
which reported a 42.91 million pound loss

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.490
across 268 stores in 2012 compared

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:06.990
to a 16.32 million pound
profit across 278 stores

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.550
six years later.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.270
- The two businesses
are on paper identical.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.210
It had had identical histories

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.600
and had reached an
identical point of failure.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:19.710
- [Narrator] That's why Barnes &amp; Noble

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:21.030
relinquished some control

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.370
to individual stores, but that comes

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.710
with its own set of
challenges for the company.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.930
- You could walk into a
bookstore and find out

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:29.310
that it's really not doing well.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.470
The manager's choices aren't appealing

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.150
to customers and books aren't selling.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.470
- [Narrator] Still Barnes &amp; Noble says

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:36.120
the strategy is promising.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.100
For the first time in more than a decade,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.120
the company is planning

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.960
on opening more stores
than it closes in 2023.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.480
- We've seen a balancing in
which a much higher percentage

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:46.920
of our sales are coming from books

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.440
and books are growing
really very dramatically.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.416
If you run better bookstores,
you sell more books.

00:05:52.416 --> 00:05:55.083
(pensive music)

